{
  // Cloudflare Workers configuration for the DealFlow AI web app.
  //
  // Deploy target: STAGED on *.workers.dev first, then cut over to the apex
  // (dealswiftautomation.com) + www (www 301s to apex — csrfProtection.ts
  // compares hosts). When the apex route is added below, it REPLACES the DNS
  // records serving WordPress — that is the cutover moment. Until then, no
  // "routes" entry keeps the deploy on workers.dev and the live site untouched.
  // See "Cloudflare Workers deploy" in apps/web/DEPLOY.md for the runbook.
  //
  // `main` points at OUR custom worker, not the OpenNext-generated one: the
  // generated worker only exports `fetch`, and Cloudflare Cron Triggers need a
  // `scheduled` handler. custom-worker.ts re-exports the generated fetch handler
  // and adds the scheduled one.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "dealswift-app",
  "main": "./custom-worker.ts",
  // Set to today's date when first deploying, per Cloudflare guidance.
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-16",
  // nodejs_compat gives workerd the Node.js APIs Next.js server runtime needs.
  // Deliberately NOT enabling enable_nodejs_tcp_sockets: SMTP (nodemailer) is
  // not viable on Workers (port 25 is blocked) and email runs over the SES
  // HTTPS API instead. See "Cloudflare Workers deploy / Known constraints"
  // in apps/web/DEPLOY.md.
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  // NO `limits` BLOCK — DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and that is load-bearing.
  //
  // Do not add `limits.cpu_ms` here. It was tried on 2026-10-03 and Cloudflare
  // REJECTED the deploy outright:
  //
  //   "CPU limits are not supported for the Free plan."
  //   [code: 100328]
  //
  // This account is on the Workers **Free** plan, despite DEPLOY.md's
  // "Prerequisites" claiming Paid. On Free the per-invocation CPU budget is
  // fixed and cannot be raised, so the budget must instead be RESPECTED.
  //
  // * RSC prefetching — 18+ parallel server-component renders blew the budget
  //   and returned 503. Fixed by prefetch={false} (measured 16% 503 -> 0%).
  //
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SIGN-IN 503 — THE ORIGINAL CLAIM WAS CORRECT. An interim "correction" on
// 2026-10-03 was WRONG and has been removed. Full account below.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// The note previously here read:
//
//   "PASSWORD HASHING — STILL UNRESOLVED. POST /api/auth/sign-up/email
//    measures ok=3 / err=3 over six attempts: roughly half of all signup and
//    signin attempts 503... argon2 is deliberately CPU-expensive and it does
//    not fit a Free-plan budget."
//
// An interim edit claimed this was "really" a 429 rate limit and that no plan
// upgrade was justified. THAT WAS WRONG, and it was wrong because it was
// concluded from sequential probes only. Under sequential load the failure is
// often a 429; under the concurrency a real browser produces, it is a 503.
//
// MEASURED 2026-10-03 (scripts/_signin-503-rootcause.mjs, 8 attempts, 11s
// apart so the rate limiter can never be what is observed):
//
//   #0..#7  status=503  content-type=text/html  body 4738 bytes
//           <title>Worker exceeded resource limits</title>
//           cf-ray a44d6f841f8f..., a44d6fca7f96..., a44d70109eb4..., ...
//
// 8/8 Cloudflare CPU aborts. That is Cloudflare's own error page, not an
// application response, and it names the failure outright.
//
// DISCRIMINATOR (scripts/_control-during-503.mjs, run while sign-in was failing):
//
//   signin      -> 503 "Worker exceeded resource limits"  (and some THROW)
//   get-session -> 200 x4        (touches the same DB, never hashes)
//   health      -> 200 x4        (touches the same DB, never hashes)
//   /           -> 500/503
//
// The DB-backed endpoints that do NOT hash stay healthy while the hashing one
// dies. That is the signature of CPU being spent in argon2, exactly as the
// original note said.
//
// THE 429 IS ALSO REAL, AND THAT IS WHAT MISLED THE INTERIM EDIT:
//   better-auth ships a built-in brute-force guard this repo never configures
//   (src/lib/auth.ts has no `rateLimit` block). From the installed source,
//   node_modules/better-auth/dist/api/rate-limiter/index.mjs ->
//   getDefaultSpecialRules():  /sign-in*, /sign-up*, /change-password*,
//   /change-email*  -> 3 per 10s. Measured 12 sequential -> 3x200 / 9x429.
//   So sequential probes measure the RATE LIMITER, and concurrent probes
//   measure the CPU CEILING. Both are real. They were conflated.
//
// => STATUS: UNRESOLVED, and it is a genuine production blocker. Password
//    hashing does not fit the Free-plan CPU budget.
//
// => The two real options are unchanged, and neither is a code tweak:
//      1. Upgrade the account to Workers Paid (a billing decision, owner's).
//      2. Stop hashing with argon2 and use better-auth's cheaper default,
//         keeping argon2 verify only for legacy hashes. That is an explicit
//         security downgrade and must be DECIDED, not slipped in.
//    Do not "fix" it by loosening auth, disabling the hash, or caching the
//    result: argon2's cost IS its security property.
//
// RELATED OPEN GAPS, both measured 2026-10-03 and pinned by
// src/app/api/utils/deployed-signin-throttle.test.ts:
//   * The sign-in limiter is NOT atomic. burst=8 -> 5 admitted against a
//     documented max of 3, because better-auth does read-decide-write around
//     awaits. The brute-force guard is bypassable by parallelising attempts.
//   * GET / intermittently 500s with "TypeError: components.ComponentMod.handler
//     is not a function" (captured live via `wrangler tail`, cf-ray
//     a44dae47afab3f27). NOT reproduced under controlled load: 40/40 clean
//     across idle, tight-sequential and 8-way parallel phases
//     (scripts/_load-correlation.mjs). Cause UNIDENTIFIED. Do not record it as
//     the CPU ceiling without capturing the Cloudflare error code first
//     (scripts/_cf-error-code.mjs).
  //
  // See UNIFIED-REQUIREMENTS-MATRIX.md for the measurements.
  // Static assets emitted by the OpenNext build. Requests to assets are free and
  // unlimited on every Workers plan — this is what gives us unmetered bandwidth.
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  // Replaces apps/web/vercel.json crons. Workers Free allows 5 cron triggers per
  // ACCOUNT, so this stays at 3. Cron expressions are UTC.
  //
  // Keep in sync with routeForCron() in custom-worker.ts.
  "triggers": {
    "crons": [
      "*/5 * * * *", // job queue drain (speed-to-lead SLA)
      "*/15 * * * *", // pipeline orchestration + daily digests
      "0 8 * * *" // end-of-day drain sweep
    ]
  }
}
